  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a multi-stage optimization algorithm combining global search and local refinement to escape local optima. Unlike the original method, this approach uses: (1) a random search at coarse grid to find promising regions (2) iterative refinement through CMA-ES (evolutionary strategy) to optimize in higher resolutions (3) hybrid local search to polish the solution. This method explicitly targets the challenging boundary regions in the optimization landscape that local methods often miss.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe optimization problem:\nMinimize C_5(h) = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\nsum(h_i) * (2.0/n) = 1\n\nWe represent h as a vector of n samples at equally spaced points in [0,2]. The objective is evaluated via:\n  dx = 2.0/n\n  h_feas = project_feasible(h, n/2)\n  correlations = np.correlate(h_feas, 1-h_feas, mode='full')\n  C_5_value = float(np.max(correlations)) * dx\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initial setup:\n   - Parameters: n_grid_init = 100, n_inner = 300, n_fine = 400\n   - Random seed initialization for reproducibility\n   - Budget tracking to adjust stages\n\n2. Coarse Global Search (Stages 1-3):\n   For iteration in range(n_grid_init):\n      a. Generate random sample at current grid size (starts at None)\n      b. Perform feasibility projection (clipping, sum adjustment)\n      c. Evaluate C_5\n      d. Track best solution so far\n      e. Gradually increase grid size while preserving best solution\n      f. Apply early termination if budget exceeds\n\n3. Evolutionary Refinement (Stage 4+):\n   Once grid is set to n_inner (\u2248200 points):\n      a. Apply CMA-ES optimization with:\n         - Covariance matrix adaptation\n         - Bound handling via box constraints\n         - Feasibility projection post-evaluation\n         - Dimension-dependent parameters (higher learning rate for first evaluations)\n\n4. Local Polishing (Stage 5+):\n   After sufficient refinement:\n      a. Switch to gradient-based optimization (SLSQP)\n      b. Gradually reduce random perturbations\n      c. Apply worst-lag surgery refinement\n      d. Finalize with subgradient polishing\n\n5. Solution handling:\n   - Maintain history of best candidates across grids\n   - Validate all candidates for constraints\n   - Return solution at grid refinement should exceed budget\n   - Automatic hybridization of methods\n\nImplementation details:\n- Coarse grid: n = 50, increases to 250\n- Grid transfer: interpolate_to() preserves continuity\n- Project_feasible() ensures constraints\n- Objective calculation uses compute_corr()\n\n- CMA-ES implementation:\n   * Handles dimensionality via covariance matrix\n   * Requires Jacobian/fun eval (we compute finite differences)\n   * Stochastic exit with best candidate storage\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Grid progression: geometric increase from 50 to 250\n- Mutation rate: 0.1 in early stages, decreases to 0.03\n- Temperature parameter: starts at 100, adapts based on improvement\n- Population size: varies with dimension (n^(0.5) scaling)\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- Primary time allocation: 40% CMA-ES, 30% SLSQP 25% C5, 5% random\n- Feasibility checks at each evaluation \n- Progress logging and budget adaptation\n- Fallback: latest feasible solution\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- All solutions satisfy: sum(h)\u2248n/2 (within 1e-6)\n- All h[i] in [0,1] post-projection\n- Objective evaluated via compute_c5()\n- Final evaluation after all operations",
